meta {
  name: Update Primary Contact
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/contacts/{{primaryContactId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

script:pre-request {
  /*
   * The surname is derived from the contact being edited, so this request is
   * re-runnable against a database it has already been run against.
   *
   * Since PAC-91 §9 a contact is unique on name + DOB + phone-or-email, so a
   * *fixed* name here made the request self-colliding: the first run renamed
   * whichever contact `Get Lead` had captured, and the next run — which
   * captures a different one, because the Create Lead requests above have since
   * added leads — asked the API to create a second person with that identity
   * and correctly got a 409. Deriving it means two contacts never collide, and
   * re-editing the same contact is excluded from its own check.
   */
  bru.setVar(
    "editedContactSurname",
    "Vaidya-" + String(bru.getVar("primaryContactId")).slice(-6),
  );
}

body:json {
  {
    "firstName": "Anurodh",
    "lastName": "{{editedContactSurname}}",
    "dateOfBirth": "1978-04-12",
    "email": "anurodh.vaidya@example.com",
    "phone": "(512) 884-3920"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the updated contact", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.id).to.equal(bru.getVar("primaryContactId"));
    expect(body.name).to.equal("Anurodh " + bru.getVar("editedContactSurname"));
    // `role` and `isPrimary` are always null/false here since PAC-91 §5: both
    // are facts about a *membership*, and this endpoint edits the person with
    // no household in hand. The Lead Detail page reads them from
    // `GET /leads/:id`, which resolves them against the lead's household.
    expect(body.role).to.equal(null);
    expect(body.isPrimary).to.equal(false);
  });

  test("date of birth is a calendar date, not a timestamp", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().dateOfBirth).to.equal("1978-04-12");
  });

  test("phone is normalized to digits for contact matching", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().phone).to.equal("5128843920");
  });

  test("leaks no internals", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("agencyId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("branchId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("householdId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("legacySmartSuiteId");
    expect(body).to.not.have.property("isTestRecord");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /contacts/:id — edit a lead's primary contact (PAC-38)

  Backs the "Edit Primary Contact" modal on the Lead Detail page: first name,
  last name, date of birth, email and phone.

  - **Module:** `clients` (must be enabled for the agency)
  - **Permission:** `clients:write`
  - **Data scope:** `own` is **derived** — see below.

  ## Why this lives under `clients`, not `leads`

  A `Contact` is a CRM record shared across every lead and policy in a
  household, so routing its writes through the `leads` module would misfile
  them. PAC-38 accordingly added `clients:write` to the **Producer** role
  template, which previously granted no `clients:*` at all.

  Existing agencies do not pick that up automatically: `seedDefaultRoles` only
  runs for agencies someone re-seeds. Run `npm run api:sync:roles:dev` — and note
  a producer who is already signed in keeps their cached permission set until
  they log in again.

  ## The scope clamp is not the module gate

  `Contact` carries **no `producerId`** and no lead reference, so the module
  permission alone would hand a producer the entire agency's client book.
  `ContactAccessService` therefore *derives* ownership: under `DataScope.own`
  the caller must own a lead that reaches this contact, either directly
  (`primaryContactId` / `memberContactIds`) or through the contact's household.
  That is exactly the roster the Lead Detail page renders.

  `branch` callers are clamped on `branchId` directly; `agency` callers reach
  every contact. Failures are `404`, never `403` — see the companion
  **(Foreign Contact)** request.

  ## Path params
  | param | type   | notes                                               |
  |-------|--------|-----------------------------------------------------|
  | id    | string | ObjectId. A malformed id is a `404`, never a `500`. |

  ## Body
  | field       | type            | rules                                       |
  |-------------|-----------------|---------------------------------------------|
  | firstName   | string          | 1–60 chars                                  |
  | lastName    | string          | 1–60 chars                                  |
  | dateOfBirth | string \| null  | `YYYY-MM-DD`; `null` clears                 |
  | email       | string \| null  | valid email, max 160; `null` clears         |
  | phone       | string \| null  | 10–20 chars; `null` clears                  |

  At least one must be present; `{}` is a `400`.

  Every field is optional **and** the last three are nullable — deliberately
  laxer than `POST /leads`, which requires DOB, email and phone. A migrated
  contact frequently has none of them, and demanding a date of birth in order to
  fix a typo'd surname would force the producer to invent data.

  ## Behaviour worth knowing

  **Email and phone are normalized through the intake helpers** (lowercase;
  digits-only) rather than a local re-implementation. Contact matching compares
  stored values against those exact shapes, so a divergent rule here would
  silently break dedupe on the next lead for the same person.

  **One email and one phone.** A contact is one person (PAC-91 §1); these were
  `string[]` mirroring SmartSuite's field type, and the edit replaced only
  element 0. The edit now simply sets the value.

  **The identity keys are re-stamped.** `nameKey` / `dobKey` are derived by a
  schema hook on save, so changing the name or date of birth moves the contact
  in the §9 unique indexes rather than leaving it there under its old identity.

  **An edit that would create a duplicate is refused.** Same full name, same
  date of birth, and the same phone *or* email as another contact in the agency
  is the same person by the owner's rule, so the request returns **409** with
  that contact's `contactId` rather than storing a second copy of them.

  **The name is mirrored onto the lead.** `Lead` still duplicates `firstName` /
  `lastName` — a lead can exist before anyone has decided which contact it
  belongs to — and the Leads list, its search and the detail header read the
  *lead's* copy, so without the mirror a corrected surname would still show the
  old value on `/leads` forever. Scoped to leads this contact is **primary**
  for: editing a spouse or child touches no lead. Email and phone are **not**
  mirrored, because the lead no longer has anywhere to put them — every reader
  follows `primaryContactId` to this contact, so the edit is visible everywhere
  the moment it is saved.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "id": "665f...b02",
    "firstName": "Anurodh",
    "lastName": "Vaidya-501caeb",
    "name": "Anurodh Vaidya-501caeb",
    "dateOfBirth": "1978-04-12",
    "email": "anurodh.vaidya@example.com",
    "phone": "5128843920",
    "role": null,
    "isPrimary": false
  }
  ```

  `role` and `isPrimary` are **always** `null` / `false` on this response
  (PAC-91 §5). Both belong to a *membership* — the same person is a Named
  Insured at home and a Driver on a parent's policy — and this endpoint edits
  the person, with no household in hand. `GET /leads/:id` resolves both against
  the lead's household, which is where the page reads them from; nothing on
  screen depends on them here.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — empty body, a malformed `dateOfBirth`, an invalid email.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `clients` module disabled, or missing `clients:write`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such contact, a malformed id, or a contact the caller
    cannot reach from one of their own leads.
  - `409 Conflict` — the edit would make this contact a duplicate of another
    under the identity rule (PAC-91 §9). The body carries `contactId` and
    `householdIds` (a list since PAC-91 §5) so the UI can offer the existing
    contact instead.

  > **Tip:** run **Get Lead** first — it captures `primaryContactId` into a
  > runtime var that this request consumes.
}
